  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nNot a lot of change in the forecast evolution for Sunday with\nthe latest model suite. It appears that flash flooding would get\nthe nod over severe weather if you had to put the threats in \norder. \n\nFlash flooding:\nA plume of high PWATS (in excess of 2.00\") will overspread most\nof the region Sunday with high confidence. Meanwhile, the\nvertically stacked cyclone allows southerly wind flow to become\nparallel to the baroclinic zone, setting the stage for training.\nHREF 95th percentile precip features pockets of over 4\" across\nparts of northwest PA, while LPMM has several pockets of 3\". We\ncan't take the locations lock step, but this does highlight the\npotential to see a 10-20 year rainfall return interval.\nProbability of 3\" or more of rain in 24h from the HREF with a\n25-km neighborhood radius features several relative maxima,\nincluding along an axis from YNG-FKL (where exceedance probs of\n60-70% are noted) and from MGW-AOO (where 60-80% exceedance\nprobs are noted). All this said, we may need to hoist a short-\nfuse flood watch as this event unfolds. The area of most concern\nwould be northwestern PA given paintball plots of highest QPF. \n\nThere will be upper-level support through ageostrophic wind \ndivergence at H2 thanks to favorable jet location. It will be an\nefficient rain process with warm cloud depths over 11-12KFT. \nInterestingly enough, the probability of 1\" per hour rain rates\nare low, which suggests repeated rounds of storms could lead to\nstreams coming out of their banks and/or road closures. With \nthe dry run recently, flash flood guidance for three hours is \nrunning around 2.00\" while the urban guidance of 0.75\" in one \nhour remains the same. \n\nSevere thunderstorms:\nThe trends continues toward a less-favorable shear profile for\npotential weak tornadoes given straight-line hodographs and SRH\nvalues. Friday it was because of a veer-back-veer setup and\nSunday will reflect weaker speed shear. Nonetheless, a damaging\nwind threat does exist given 30-35 knots of deep-layer shear and\nMLCAPE around 1000 J/kg. The coverage/intensity of storms\ndepends on how much diurnal heating we observe tomorrow. Looking\nupstream over parts of IND ad KY where the dry slot is allowing\nfor breaks in the clouds, extrapolation brings that across \neastern Ohio and possibly into northern WV and western PA Sunday\nafternoon. This would create sufficient heating to fire off \nstorms that could knock down trees and wires. The way the CAMs \nare playing things, I could see a scenario where the severe \nthunderstorm potential resides over our Ohio counties and the \nexcessive rain threat stays in northwest PA. Either way, there \nwill be a break between showers overnight and the storms Sunday \nafternoon. The longer the break and more sunshine we see, the \nhigher chance of storms, some of which could be severe. \n\nMachine learning is mostly in line with SPC with Nadocast \nputting a 15% chance of severe wind over western PA and the \nnorthern WV Panhandle. Depending on the how much we clear out \nand how long goes a long way to putting the pieces of this \natmospheric puzzle together. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nThe slow-moving upper-level low finally kicks out of here mid \nweek, however broad troughing over the Great Lakes quickly \nestablishes. This spells a forecast with a continued chance of \nshowers and thunderstorms every day next week. The time window \nwhen they are most likely to happen is during peak heating \nhours. As stated in the key messages above, no all-day rains \nare forecast, and some locations may not see a drop all week. \nThere will be periods of sunshine as well, so don't put away the\nsunglasses just because you see a chance of rain in the \nforecast. \n\nTemperatures will be above average all week as humidity returns.\nDaytime highs will be widespread in the 80s with some\ncommunities hitting 90F in West Virginia and eastern Ohio.\nOvernight lows won't get below 65 unless you reside north of I80\nor in the mountains of southwestern PA and northern WV. As of\nnow, no organized severe weather is expected.",
